# Wrangler `.dev.vars` template — copy to `.dev.vars` for local development.
#
# `wrangler dev` (including the workerd-backed `pnpm dev` via
# `@cloudflare/vite-plugin`) auto-loads this file from the project root
# and exposes each entry as a Worker binding, mirroring `wrangler secret
# put` for production. Never commit the real `.dev.vars` — it is listed
# in `.gitignore`.
#
# Non-secret config (e.g. `APP_URL`) belongs in the matching
# `wrangler*.toml` `[vars]`, not here — `wrangler.toml` for local dev,
# `wrangler.staging.toml` / `wrangler.production.toml` for remote
# deploys. Reserve this file for credentials and tokens that must not
# be checked in.
#
# HMAC key that signs session cookies. Left empty on purpose: a value
# published in this file would be a valid key for anyone who reads the
# repository, and copying it into a deployment hands out every account.
# Generate your own — the Worker refuses every request until you do:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters. Deliberately a secret rather than a `[vars]`
# entry — `wrangler.toml` does not inherit `[vars]` across `[env.*]`
# blocks, so a var would have to be duplicated per environment in
# plaintext. For deployed environments use `wrangler secret put
# SESSION_SECRET`. Only the request-path Worker needs it.
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Example (uncomment and replace when you add a secret-backed feature):
# SOME_THIRD_PARTY_API_KEY="replace-me"
